---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'requirements', label: 'WCAG requirements' },
   { id: 'screen-reader', label: 'Screen reader output' },
   { id: 'axe', label: 'axe runs' },
   { id: 'coverage', label: 'Coverage' },
   { id: 'agents', label: 'Agent tools' },
   { id: 'interfaces', label: 'Interfaces' },
   { id: 'next', label: 'Start here' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Overview"
   summary="A site's accessibility gets measured against a set of published requirements. An automated scan can decide some of them by reading a page's HTML. The rest need someone to listen to the page the way a screen reader user does. a11ied runs both kinds of check from one command line, one MCP server, and one TypeScript API."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="requirements">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Requirements</p>
         <h2>WCAG requirements</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         The <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/">Web Content
         Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG)</a> is the standard most accessibility
         requirements point back to. It groups its rules into numbered, testable success
         criteria, each at level A, AA, or AAA.
      </p>
      <p>
         <a href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core">axe-core (axe)</a> is the
         open-source rules engine most automated accessibility scans run. It reads a
         page's rendered HTML and decides the success criteria it can decide on its own.
         <code>a1 axe</code> runs it against a URL.
      </p>
      <p>
         The rest need a person to listen to the page. <a
         href="https://support.apple.com/guide/voiceover/welcome/mac">VoiceOver</a> on
         macOS and <a href="https://www.nvaccess.org/about-nvda/">NVDA</a> on Windows are
         the screen readers people actually use. <code>a1 sr</code> drives either one,
         plus a simulated screen reader that needs neither installed, and returns what it
         announced as text.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="screen-reader">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Screen reader output</p>
         <h2>The announcement order for a page</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>sr walk</code> starts a session, reads the whole page top to bottom, and
         returns the spoken text as JSON, one entry per item.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr walk --sr virtual --allow-virtual http://localhost:3000/checkout --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"spokenPhraseLog": [
  "document",
  "heading, Checkout, level 1",
  "form",
  "Email",
  "textbox, Email, required",
  "Card number",
  "textbox, Card number",
  "button, Pay now",
  "button",
  "end of form",
  "end of document"
]</code></pre>
      <p>
         The log is the order a screen reader user moves through. This one shows the required state
         on the email field, and a tenth entry that announces as <code>button</code> with no name.
      </p>
      <p>
         Swap <code>--sr virtual --allow-virtual</code> for <code>--sr voiceover</code> to run the
         same walk against VoiceOver. See <a href="/screen-readers">screen readers</a> for NVDA and
         when to use each one.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="axe">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">axe runs</p>
         <h2>An axe run against a URL</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>--level AA</code> resolves the 73 axe rules mapped to WCAG 2.2 levels A and AA
         and runs them against the page.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 axe http://localhost:3000/checkout --level AA --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"verdict": &#123;
  "totalViolationNodes": 1,
  "failingFindings": [
    &#123; "ruleId": "button-name", "impact": "critical", "target": ["button[type=\"button\"]"] &#125;
  ],
  "passed": false
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
         This page fails: one element trips <code>button-name</code>, the rule that checks a
         button has text a screen reader can announce. The full result also lists every rule axe
         ran and passed, so a clean run states exactly what it checked.
      </p>
      <p>
         Scope a run three other ways: <code>--criterion 1.4.3</code> for one success
         criterion, <code>--rule color-contrast --rule link-name</code> for named rules, or no
         selector at all for every rule mapped in the WCAG version.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="coverage">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Coverage</p>
            <h2>Criteria axe cannot decide</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            Coverage says whether axe can decide a success criterion on its own, or whether a
            person has to check it too. a11ied derives coverage from the W3C ACT rule mapping and
            the axe rule tags, both included as local data, so a lookup makes no network request.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 wcag 2.4.3 --json</code></pre>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"coverage": &#123;
  "coverageState": "hybrid",
  "axeRuleIds": [],
  "actRuleIds": [],
  "notes": ["Focus order depends on interaction flow, not just static structure."]
&#125;,
"strategy": &#123;
  "preferredEvidenceMode": "hybrid",
  "procedureIds": ["focus_order_probe"],
  "requiresRealTarget": true
&#125;</code></pre>
         <p>
            No axe rule decides Focus Order on its own. The strategy lists the procedure to
            run, <code>focus_order_probe</code>, and says the test needs a real target, which a
            driver session provides. <a href="/test-methods">Test methods</a> explains the four
            states and where the mapping comes from.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">WCAG 2.2, level AA</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            24 success criteria. axe rules decide 8. Four are hybrid. Twelve stay manual.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="agents">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Agent tools</p>
         <h2>The same commands for a coding agent</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         The MCP server runs over stdio and opens no network port. Add it to any MCP client and the
         agent gets criterion lookup, coverage lookup, axe runs, and screen reader sessions.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="CONFIG"><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "a11ied": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "a11ied", "mcp"]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Test http://localhost:3000/checkout with a11ied.
Run axe at level AA, then step through the form with the screen reader.
Report the axe violations and the spoken phrase log separately.</code></pre>
      <p>
         The <a href="/guides/agent-skill">agent skill</a> adds the reasoning: check coverage first,
         pick the right target, and keep automated results apart from transcripts.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="interfaces">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Interfaces</p>
         <h2>Ways to use a11ied</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/reference/cli">CLI</a></strong>
            <span>The <code>a1</code> command. Most subcommands take <code>--json</code> and print one structured object. <code>mcp</code> and <code>help-all</code> print plain text only.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/reference/mcp">MCP server</a></strong>
            <span>13 tools and seven read-only resources over stdio, for coding agents and editors.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/reference/api">TypeScript API</a></strong>
            <span>Import <code>runAxe</code>, <code>startDriverSession</code>, and the WCAG lookups from <code>a11ied</code>.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <p>
         The MCP tools and the CLI commands call the same functions. A result from one matches a
         result from any other.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="next">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Start here</p>
         <h2>Your first test</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         The <a href="/quickstart">quickstart</a> installs the package, checks your machine, and
         takes one local route from an axe run to a screen reader transcript in about five
         minutes.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
